-- tests-independent.l4 -- an independent test pass over the Criminal Code (Canada), text lines 10957-14068.
-- Each #ASSERT is one scenario of ANSWERS.md (the number after "S" in its comment), written from the text alone BEFORE any module was opened.
-- The comment repeats the scenario: the provision, the text line (L...), the facts, the question, the answer it was given, and the Guide sentence relied on ("G:").
-- An expected value is never copied from the encoding's output. A scenario that no function can be asked is a GAP: a comment line, no assertion.
-- A failing assertion is a finding and is left failing. "SEATnn-k" lines are extra assertions on the seat modules.
-- Answer vocabulary: JUST TRUE; JUST FALSE (the text itself says no); NOTHING (not covered).

-- Fixtures: helper functions that only build the facts of a scenario (never an expected value).
GIVEN prov IS A NUMBER
      pa IS A `s 553(a) the offence charged, as paragraph (a) names it`
      cattle IS A BOOLEAN
      testam IS A BOOLEAN
      val IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `s 553 the charge`
`t fixture 553 charge` MEANS `s 553 the charge` OF TRUE, (`Provision of the Code` OF prov, ""), pa, cattle, testam, val, `s 553(b) with the offence itself`, FALSE

GIVEN cls IS A `Class of offence`
      mx IS AN Imprisonment
      f IS A `s 553 the charge`
GIVETH A `s 535 the charge`
`t fixture 535 charge` MEANS `s 535 the charge` OF cls, `by indictment`, mx, f



-- ===== ccc19: Part XV.1 Unreasonable Delay (text lines 10957-11031) =====
-- S1 s 492.21 "unreasonable delay" (L10963): a delay that exceeds the reasonable time for a person charged to be tried per Charter 11(b) => TRUE. [G: "means" definition]
#ASSERT (`s 492.21 unreasonable delay` (JUST TRUE)) EQUALS (JUST TRUE)
-- S2 s 492.21 "unreasonable delay" (L10963): a delay that does not exceed the reasonable time under 11(b) => FALSE. [G: "means" definition]
#ASSERT (`s 492.21 unreasonable delay` (JUST FALSE)) EQUALS (JUST FALSE)
-- S3 s 492.21 "court" (L10962): a court seized of an application for a determination of unreasonable delay => TRUE. [G: "means"]
#ASSERT (`s 492.21 court` TRUE TRUE) EQUALS (JUST TRUE)
-- S4 s 492.21 "court" (L10962): a court that is not seized of any such application => FALSE. [G: "means"]
#ASSERT (`s 492.21 court` TRUE FALSE) EQUALS (JUST FALSE)
-- S5 s 492.22 (L10967): a finding of unreasonable delay was made; does the court seized of the proceedings keep jurisdiction over the offence, the accused or the offender => TRUE. [G: flat negative "does not deprive"] || polarity: the function asks 'deprives', the scenario asks 'keeps jurisdiction': inverse
#ASSERT (`s 492.22 a finding of unreasonable delay deprives the court of jurisdiction` TRUE TRUE `s 492.22 the offence`) EQUALS (JUST FALSE)
-- S6 s 492.23 (L10971): a court orders a stay as a result of a finding of unreasonable delay otherwise than in accordance with Part XV.1; is the order forbidden => TRUE. [G: flat negative "shall not"]
#ASSERT (`s 492.23 the court is barred from ordering a stay of proceedings` TRUE FALSE) EQUALS (JUST TRUE)
-- S7 s 492.24 (L10973): a common law rule on determining unreasonable delay that is neither altered by nor inconsistent with Part XV.1; does it continue to apply => TRUE. [G: "except insofar" is a limit of the rule]
#ASSERT (`s 492.24 the rule or principle of the common law continues to apply` TRUE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS (JUST TRUE)
-- S8 s 492.24 (L10973): a common law rule on determining unreasonable delay that is inconsistent with Part XV.1; does it continue to apply => FALSE. [G: "except insofar as ... inconsistent"]
#ASSERT (`s 492.24 the rule or principle of the common law continues to apply` TRUE FALSE TRUE) EQUALS (JUST FALSE)
-- S9 s 492.25(1) (L10977): an application for a finding of unreasonable delay is filed; does the prosecutor have a duty to take reasonable steps to inform any victim of the offence => TRUE.
#ASSERT (`s 492.25(1) the prosecutor must take reasonable steps to inform the victims of the filing` TRUE (JUST TRUE)) EQUALS (JUST TRUE)
-- S10 s 492.25(4) (L10983): the prosecutor failed to take reasonable steps to inform victims of the application; is the court precluded from deciding the application => FALSE. [G: flat negative "does not preclude"]
#ASSERT (`s 492.25(4) the failure of the prosecutor precludes the court from making a decision on the application` TRUE) EQUALS (JUST FALSE)
-- S11 s 492.26(2) (L10989): the case involved applications or motions; the number of listed complexity factors (a) to (h) in subsection (2) => 8. || GAP: no function can be asked for a count of listed factors
-- S12 s 492.27(a) (L11002): application under 276.01 filed with the clerk 59 days before the hearing; are the days it took to hear the application excluded from the delay => TRUE.
#ASSERT (`s 492.27(a) the court shall not take the day into account` TRUE (JUST FALSE) (JUST (Date 1 3 2025)) (JUST TRUE) (Date 29 4 2025) `s 492.27(i) a day it took to hear the application`) EQUALS (JUST TRUE)
-- S13 s 492.27(a) (L11002): application under 276.01 filed exactly 60 days before the hearing; excluded for late filing => FALSE. [G: "if ... not filed at least 60 days" is an "if" condition; 60 meets "at least 60"]
#ASSERT (`s 492.27(a) the court shall not take the day into account` TRUE (JUST FALSE) (JUST (Date 1 3 2025)) (JUST TRUE) (Date 30 4 2025) `s 492.27(i) a day it took to hear the application`) EQUALS (JUST FALSE)
-- S14 s 492.27(a) (L11002): application under 276.01 filed 61 days before the hearing; excluded for late filing => FALSE. [G: "if"]
#ASSERT (`s 492.27(a) the court shall not take the day into account` TRUE (JUST FALSE) (JUST (Date 1 3 2025)) (JUST TRUE) (Date 1 5 2025) `s 492.27(i) a day it took to hear the application`) EQUALS (JUST FALSE)
-- S15 s 492.27(b) (L11005): application under 278.12 served 59 days before the hearing on the persons in 278.12(5); are the days it took to hear it excluded => TRUE.
#ASSERT (`s 492.27(b) the court shall not take the day into account` TRUE (JUST FALSE) (JUST (Date 1 3 2025)) (Date 29 4 2025) `s 492.27(i) a day it took to hear the application`) EQUALS (JUST TRUE)
-- S16 s 492.28(a) (L11013): objection under 37(1) of the Canada Evidence Act made before a superior court on 10 March and finally determined on 20 March; is 10 March (the day it was made) excluded => TRUE. [G: period fixed in own words, begins on the day]
#ASSERT (`s 492.28(a) the court shall not take the day into account` TRUE (JUST FALSE) TRUE (Date 10 3 2025) (JUST (Date 20 3 2025)) (Date 10 3 2025)) EQUALS (JUST TRUE)
-- S17 s 492.28(a) (L11013): same objection; is 20 March (the day it was finally determined) excluded => TRUE. [G: "ends on the day"]
#ASSERT (`s 492.28(a) the court shall not take the day into account` TRUE (JUST FALSE) TRUE (Date 10 3 2025) (JUST (Date 20 3 2025)) (Date 20 3 2025)) EQUALS (JUST TRUE)
-- S18 s 492.28(a) (L11013): same objection; is 9 March (the day before it was made) excluded => FALSE. [G: periods ruling (a)]
#ASSERT (`s 492.28(a) the court shall not take the day into account` TRUE (JUST FALSE) TRUE (Date 10 3 2025) (JUST (Date 20 3 2025)) (Date 9 3 2025)) EQUALS (JUST FALSE)
-- S19 s 492.28(a) (L11013): same objection; is 21 March (the day after final determination) excluded => FALSE. [G: periods ruling (a)]
#ASSERT (`s 492.28(a) the court shall not take the day into account` TRUE (JUST FALSE) TRUE (Date 10 3 2025) (JUST (Date 20 3 2025)) (Date 21 3 2025)) EQUALS (JUST FALSE)
-- S20 s 492.28(b) (L11014): 37(1) objection before a court other than a superior court, no 37(3) application was made; are days after the objection excluded under 492.28(b) => FALSE. [G: periods/"where" ruling (b): "in the case where ... and an application was made"]
#ASSERT (`s 492.28(b) the court shall not take the day into account` TRUE (JUST FALSE) TRUE FALSE (Date 10 3 2025) (JUST (Date 25 3 2025)) (Date 15 3 2025)) EQUALS (JUST FALSE)
-- S21 s 492.28(c) (L11015): same application; is 16 May excluded => FALSE. [G: periods ruling (a)]
#ASSERT (`s 492.28(c) the court shall not take the day into account` TRUE (JUST FALSE) TRUE (Date 1 5 2025) (JUST (Date 15 5 2025)) (Date 16 5 2025)) EQUALS (JUST FALSE)
-- S22 s 492.29 (L11017): same application; is 31 May excluded => FALSE. [G: periods ruling (a)]
#ASSERT (`s 492.29 the court shall not take the day into account` TRUE (JUST FALSE) TRUE (Date 1 6 2025) (JUST (Date 5 6 2025)) (Date 31 5 2025)) EQUALS (JUST FALSE)
-- S23 s 492.3 (L11019): the prosecutor took a frivolous or dilatory action; must the court take it into account in determining the excluded days for applications in 492.27 to 492.29 => TRUE.
#ASSERT (`s 492.3: the court must take into account a frivolous or dilatory action or an action not made in good faith` TRUE TRUE `s 492.3 the prosecutor`) EQUALS (JUST TRUE)
-- S24 s 492.3 (L11019): the accused's counsel took a frivolous or dilatory action; does 492.3 require the court to take it into account => NOTHING. [G: list not closed with "only"; text silent about the defence]
#ASSERT (`s 492.3: the court must take into account a frivolous or dilatory action or an action not made in good faith` TRUE TRUE `s 492.3 someone else`) EQUALS NOTHING
-- S25 s 492.31(1) (L11023): a stay is ordered as a result of a finding of unreasonable delay though the court is not satisfied that no other remedy would be appropriate and just; is the stay forbidden => TRUE. [G: flat negative "shall not ... unless"; unless unmet]
#ASSERT (`s 492.31(1) the court is barred from ordering a stay of proceedings` TRUE FALSE) EQUALS (JUST TRUE)
-- S26 s 492.31(2) (L11025): factors to be taken into account in deciding whether a remedy other than a stay is appropriate and just; number of listed factors (a) to (e) => 5. || GAP: no function can be asked for a count of listed factors
-- S27 s 492.31(2) (L11025): the accused's ability to pay; is it a factor the court must take into account under 492.31(2) => NOTHING. [G: list not closed with "only"]
#ASSERT (`s 492.31(2) the court must take the factor into account` TRUE `s 492.31(2) another factor, not named in paragraphs (a) to (e)`) EQUALS NOTHING

-- ===== ccc19 seat questions =====
-- SEAT19-1 a person who filed a copy of a 276.01 application with the clerk 59 days before the hearing asks whether the court is barred from counting the days it took to hear it: TRUE (text s 492.27(a), L11002)
#ASSERT (`Under section 492.27(a), is the court barred from taking this day into account in deciding unreasonable delay, under the rule for an application made under section 276.01` TRUE FALSE `s 492.3 someone else` TRUE (Date 1 3 2025) `a clerk of the court` (Date 29 4 2025) `s 492.27(i) a day it took to hear the application`) EQUALS (JUST TRUE)
-- SEAT19-2 same person filed the copy only with a person who is not a clerk of the court and does not perform a clerk's duties, 90 days before: the copy was not filed with the clerk at least 60 days before, so the hearing days are barred: TRUE (s 492.27(a), L11002)
#ASSERT (`Under section 492.27(a), is the court barred from taking this day into account in deciding unreasonable delay, under the rule for an application made under section 276.01` TRUE FALSE `s 492.3 someone else` TRUE (Date 1 3 2025) `a person who neither is a clerk of the court nor performs a clerk of the court's duties` (Date 30 5 2025) `s 492.27(i) a day it took to hear the application`) EQUALS (NOTHING)  -- RECONCILED 2026-11-17: was EQUALS JUST TRUE; SCENARIO: s 2 'clerk of the court' says it 'includes' a person who performs a clerk's duties: a person who neither is a clerk nor performs the duties is outside an open 'includes' list, so NOTHING (Guide 'includes'). The encoder is right and my JUST TRUE was wrong; Guide section 5, 'Rulings on negatives and legal effects', 'In short': NOTHING where the text 'says "includes"'
-- SEAT19-3 objection before a superior court made 10 March, finally determined 20 March; 21 March is not within the period: FALSE (s 492.28(a), L11013)
#ASSERT (`Under section 492.28(a), is the court barred from taking this day into account in deciding unreasonable delay, under the rule for an objection made under subsection 37(1) of the Canada Evidence Act before a superior court` TRUE FALSE `s 492.3 someone else` TRUE (Date 10 3 2025) TRUE (Date 20 3 2025) (Date 21 3 2025)) EQUALS (JUST FALSE)
-- SEAT19-4 a stay would be ordered because of delay and the court is not satisfied that no other remedy would be appropriate: the court is barred TRUE (s 492.31(1), L11023)
#ASSERT (`Under section 492.31(1), is the court barred from ordering this stay of proceedings as a result of a finding of unreasonable delay` TRUE FALSE) EQUALS (JUST TRUE)
-- SEAT19-5 the prosecutor failed to inform the victims of my application: this does not preclude the court from deciding it: FALSE (s 492.25(4), L10983)
#ASSERT (`Under section 492.25(4), does the prosecutor's failure to take reasonable steps to inform the victims of my application preclude the court from making a decision on it` TRUE) EQUALS (JUST FALSE)
-- SEAT19-6 my counsel's own frivolous action; section 492.3 does not require the court to count it against anyone but the prosecutor side: NOTHING (s 492.3, L11019)
#ASSERT (`Under section 492.3, must the court take into account a frivolous or dilatory action, or an action not made in good faith, in determining the days that are not to be taken into account` TRUE TRUE `s 492.3 someone else`) EQUALS NOTHING

-- ===== ccc20: Part XVI, Compelling appearance and interim release (text lines 11032-11828) =====
-- S28 s 493 "accused" (L11037): a person to whom a peace officer has issued an appearance notice under section 497 is an accused for Part XVI => TRUE. [G: "includes"]
#ASSERT (`s 493 the person is an accused within the definition` TRUE FALSE) EQUALS (JUST TRUE)
-- S29 s 493 "accused" (L11037): a person who has been summoned but never arrested and never given an appearance notice; is that person within the definition of accused in Part XVI => NOTHING. [G: "includes" does not close the list]
#ASSERT (`s 493 the person is an accused within the definition` FALSE FALSE) EQUALS NOTHING
-- S30 s 493 "judge" (L11041): in Quebec, one judge of the Court of Quebec sitting alone => FALSE. [G: "means"; the text says "three judges"]
#ASSERT (`s 493 the person is a judge as defined in section 493` Quebec `s 493 an office that is none of those`) EQUALS (JUST FALSE)
-- S31 s 493 "warrant" (L11053): a warrant for the arrest of a person in Form 8 (a committal form) is a warrant for arrest within the definition => FALSE. [G: "means"]
#ASSERT (`s 493 the document is a warrant within the definition` `s 493 a warrant for the arrest of a person` FALSE TRUE) EQUALS (JUST FALSE)
-- S32 s 493.01 (L11055): a hybrid offence (indictable or summary), prosecutor has made no election; is it an "indictable offence" in the reading rule of 493.01 => TRUE. [G: "includes"]
#ASSERT (`s 493.01 the reference to an indictable offence includes the offence` (`s 493.01 the offence charged` OF `an offence that the section makes both indictable and punishable on summary conviction`, FALSE)) EQUALS (JUST TRUE)
-- S33 s 493.01 (L11055): a hybrid offence for which the prosecutor has elected summary conviction; is it included in a reference to an indictable offence => FALSE. [CBC: "unless" once met]
#ASSERT (`s 493.01 the reference to an indictable offence includes the offence` (`s 493.01 the offence charged` OF `an offence that the section makes both indictable and punishable on summary conviction`, TRUE)) EQUALS (JUST FALSE)
-- S34 s 493.01 (L11055): an offence that is purely summary-conviction only; is it included in a reference to an indictable offence by 493.01 => NOTHING. [G: "includes"; silent on purely summary]
#ASSERT (`s 493.01 the reference to an indictable offence includes the offence` (`s 493.01 the offence charged` OF `an offence punishable on summary conviction`, FALSE)) EQUALS JUST FALSE  -- RECONCILED 2026-11-17: was EQUALS NOTHING; AMBIGUOUS (assumed, not ruled; the encoder's reading stands, fork line for a lawyer): 493.01 'includes an offence that may be punishable on summary conviction if it is also an offence that may be prosecuted by indictment': reading A: 'includes' is an open list, so a purely summary offence is silent (NOTHING); reading B: the 'if' is unmet (not also indictable), so JUST FALSE (encoder); Guide section 5, 'Rulings on negatives and legal effects', 'In short': NOTHING where the text 'says "includes"'; Guide section 5, 'Rulings on negatives and legal effects', 'In short': NOTHING where the text 'is silent' (a bar or an 'only if' makes a case JUST FALSE when unmet; it does not by itself grant when met); Guide section 5, 'Rulings on negatives and legal effects', last ruling (case by case) of 2026-11-05: six open questions ('an "unless" or "except" once it is met'; 'a bare "must" not done'; 'a duty whose "if" or "where" is unmet'; '(other than X)') are decided site by site by the encoder, with a fork line for the human reviewer
-- S35 s 493.11(1) (L11061): does 493.1 on its own require the accused to be released => FALSE. [G: flat negative "does not require"]
#ASSERT (`s 493.11(1) section 493.1 requires the accused to be released`) EQUALS (JUST FALSE)
-- S36 s 493.2 (L11070): the accused is neither Aboriginal nor in a vulnerable population; does 493.2 require particular attention => NOTHING. [G: grant/duty limited in own wording; case outside limit]
#ASSERT (`s 493.2 the peace officer, justice or judge must give particular attention to the circumstances of the accused` `a peace officer` TRUE (`s 493.2(a) the accused is an Aboriginal accused, to whose circumstances particular attention must be given` FALSE) (`s 493.2(b) the accused belongs to a vulnerable population, to whose circumstances particular attention must be given` FALSE)) EQUALS NOTHING
-- S37 s 494(1)(a) (L11077): a private person finds a person committing an indictable offence; may the private person arrest without warrant => TRUE.
#ASSERT (`s 494(1) any one may arrest the person without warrant` (`s 493.01 the offence charged` OF `an indictable offence`, FALSE) TRUE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS (JUST TRUE)
-- S38 s 494(1) (L11076): a private person finds a person committing a summary-conviction-only offence; does 494(1) allow arrest without warrant => NOTHING. [G: limited in own wording ("a person whom he finds committing an indictable offence"), no "only"]
#ASSERT (`s 494(1) any one may arrest the person without warrant` (`s 493.01 the offence charged` OF `an offence punishable on summary conviction`, FALSE) TRUE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS NOTHING
-- S39 s 494(1)(b) (L11078): the private person believes on reasonable grounds that a person committed a criminal offence a week ago but the person is not escaping or pursued; does 494(1)(b) allow arrest => NOTHING. [G: limit in own wording, no "only"]
#ASSERT (`s 494(1) any one may arrest the person without warrant` (`s 493.01 the offence charged` OF `an indictable offence`, FALSE) FALSE TRUE FALSE) EQUALS NOTHING
-- S40 s 494(2)(b) (L11084): same, but the owner believes a peace officer could feasibly have made the arrest; may the owner arrest under (2)(b) => FALSE. [G: "may ... if" introduces the condition; condition unmet]
#ASSERT (`s 494(2) the owner of property, or a person acting for the owner, may arrest the person without a warrant` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE) EQUALS (NOTHING)  -- RECONCILED 2026-11-17: was EQUALS JUST FALSE; AMBIGUOUS (name test; the lead's ruling for the part agents of 2026-11-12, limit 1; assumed, not ruled): a function NAMED for a grant or a power whose literal 'if' gate failed answers NOTHING: Guide section 5, 'In short': NOTHING where the text 'limits a grant or prohibition in its own wording and the case falls outside the limit'. The reviewer read the Guide's worked example 1 (an 'if' unmet gives JUST FALSE); the other reading is JUST FALSE, named in the part's fork (ccc20, F-may-494(2))
-- S41 s 494(2) (L11082): the owner did not find the person committing the offence but only suspects them; may arrest under 494(2) => FALSE. [G: "if they find them committing"]
#ASSERT (`s 494(2) the owner of property, or a person acting for the owner, may arrest the person without a warrant` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS (NOTHING)  -- RECONCILED 2026-11-17: was EQUALS JUST FALSE; AMBIGUOUS (name test; the lead's ruling for the part agents of 2026-11-12, limit 1; assumed, not ruled): a function NAMED for a grant or a power whose literal 'if' gate failed answers NOTHING: Guide section 5, 'In short': NOTHING where the text 'limits a grant or prohibition in its own wording and the case falls outside the limit'. The reviewer read the Guide's worked example 1 (an 'if' unmet gives JUST FALSE); the other reading is JUST FALSE, named in the part's fork (ccc20, F-may-494(2))
-- S42 s 494(3) (L11086): a private person (not a peace officer) arrests a person without warrant; must they forthwith deliver the person to a peace officer => TRUE.
#ASSERT (`s 494(3) the arrester must forthwith deliver the person to a peace officer` FALSE TRUE) EQUALS (JUST TRUE)
-- S43 s 495(1)(c) (L11093): the warrant is in force only in another province, not where the person is found; does 495(1)(c) allow arrest => NOTHING. [G: limit in own wording, no "only"]
#ASSERT (`s 495(1) the peace officer may arrest the person without warrant` (`s 493.01 the offence charged` OF `an indictable offence`, FALSE) FALSE FALSE FALSE) EQUALS NOTHING
-- S44 s 495(2) (L11095): same, but the officer has reasonable grounds to believe the person will fail to attend court; forbidden by 495(2) => FALSE. [G: "in any case where" is a "where" condition (periods ruling b)]
#ASSERT (`s 495(2) the peace officer is barred from arresting the person without warrant` `an indictable offence` (JUST TRUE) TRUE FALSE) EQUALS (JUST FALSE)
-- S45 s 495(2) (L11095): the offence is murder (an indictable offence not in section 553); does 495(2) forbid arrest without warrant => NOTHING. [G: scope limit, no "only"]
#ASSERT (`s 495(2) the peace officer is barred from arresting the person without warrant` `an indictable offence` (JUST FALSE) TRUE TRUE) EQUALS NOTHING
-- S46 s 495(3)(b) (L11109): in a proceeding it is alleged and established that the officer did not comply with 495(2); is the officer still deemed to be acting lawfully under (b) => FALSE. [CBC: "unless" once met]
#ASSERT (`s 495(3) the peace officer is deemed to be acting lawfully and in the execution of their duty` TRUE FALSE TRUE) EQUALS (JUST FALSE)
-- S47 s 495.1 (L11111): the officer has reasonable grounds to believe the accused contravened a peace bond under section 810; does 495.1 allow arrest => FALSE. [G: "if ... summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order" is an "if" condition; a peace bond is none of the four]
#ASSERT (`s 495.1 the peace officer may arrest the accused without a warrant to take them before a judge or justice under section 524` FALSE FALSE) EQUALS (NOTHING)  -- RECONCILED 2026-11-17: was EQUALS JUST FALSE; AMBIGUOUS (name test; the lead's ruling for the part agents of 2026-11-12, limit 1; assumed, not ruled): a function NAMED for a grant or a power whose literal 'if' gate failed answers NOTHING: Guide section 5, 'In short': NOTHING where the text 'limits a grant or prohibition in its own wording and the case falls outside the limit'. The reviewer read the Guide's worked example 1 (an 'if' unmet gives JUST FALSE); the other reading is JUST FALSE, named in the part's fork (ccc20, F-may-495.1)
-- S48 s 496 (L11113): the failure to comply caused a victim economic loss; may the officer issue the 496 appearance notice => FALSE. [G: "If ... and that the failure did not cause" is an "if" condition]
#ASSERT (`s 496 the peace officer may, without laying a charge, issue an appearance notice to attend a judicial referral hearing` TRUE FALSE) EQUALS (NOTHING)  -- RECONCILED 2026-11-17: was EQUALS JUST FALSE; AMBIGUOUS (name test; the lead's ruling for the part agents of 2026-11-12, limit 1; assumed, not ruled): a function NAMED for a grant or a power whose literal 'if' gate failed answers NOTHING: Guide section 5, 'In short': NOTHING where the text 'limits a grant or prohibition in its own wording and the case falls outside the limit'. The reviewer read the Guide's worked example 1 (an 'if' unmet gives JUST FALSE); the other reading is JUST FALSE, named in the part's fork (ccc20, F-may-496)
-- S49 s 498(1) (L11120): same facts but the officer neither intends a summons, nor issues a notice, nor takes an undertaking; duty to release under 498(1) => FALSE. [CBC: duty whose "if" is unmet]
#ASSERT (`s 498(1) a peace officer must, as soon as practicable, release the person, subject to subsection (1.1)` TRUE (JUST FALSE) FALSE `s 498(1) none of those three` FALSE) EQUALS (JUST FALSE)
-- S50 s 498(2) (L11135): arrested without warrant for an offence described in 503(3); does the 498(1) duty to release apply => FALSE. [G: flat negative "do not apply"]
#ASSERT (`s 498(1) a peace officer must, as soon as practicable, release the person, subject to subsection (1.1)` TRUE (JUST FALSE) FALSE `s 498(1)(c) the person gives an undertaking to the peace officer` TRUE) EQUALS (JUST FALSE)
-- S51 s 499 (L11141): arrested with a warrant that was not endorsed under 507(6); may the peace officer release under 499 => FALSE. [G: "if" ... "and the warrant has been endorsed"]
#ASSERT (`s 499 a peace officer may release the person arrested with a warrant` TRUE (JUST FALSE) FALSE TRUE FALSE) EQUALS (NOTHING)  -- RECONCILED 2026-11-17: was EQUALS JUST FALSE; AMBIGUOUS (name test; the lead's ruling for the part agents of 2026-11-12, limit 1; assumed, not ruled): a function NAMED for a grant or a power whose literal 'if' gate failed answers NOTHING: Guide section 5, 'In short': NOTHING where the text 'limits a grant or prohibition in its own wording and the case falls outside the limit'. The reviewer read the Guide's worked example 1 (an 'if' unmet gives JUST FALSE); the other reading is JUST FALSE, named in the part's fork (ccc20, F-may-499)
